<aside name="stabilityTheorem"><title>Stability theorem</title>
<p>We can summarize the results for the stability of discrete dynamical systems with the following stability theorem.</p>

<p>Consider the discrete dynamical system
<md>
  <mrow>x_{n+1}\amp = f(x_n)</mrow>
  <mrow>x_0\amp = a,</mrow>
</md>
with an 
<ref to="doenet:equilibria_discrete_dynamical_systems">equilibrium</ref><footnote>Recall that the fact that <m>E</m> is an equilibrium simply means that <m>E = f(E)</m>.
</footnote>
  <m>x_n = E</m>. Then, we can determine the stability of the equilibrium by calculating the derivative of <m>f</m> evaluated at the equilbrium as follows.</p>
<ul>
<li>If <m>|f'(E)| \lt  1</m>, then the equilibrium <m>x_n=E</m> is stable.</li>
<li>If <m>|f'(E)| \gt  1</m>, then the equilibrium <m>x_n = E</m> is unstable.</li>
<li>If <m>|f'(E)|=1</m>, then we can't figure out the stability of the equilibrium from just the derivative <m>f'(E)</m>.</li>
</ul>
</aside>


  
<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>We are given a dynamical system in function iteration form and tasked with determining its equilibria and whether the equilibria are stable.</p>
<p>The system is given by
  <me>z_{t+1} = h(z_t)</me> 
where <m>h</m> is the updating function given by

  <function name="updatingFunc" symbolic="true" variables="z" hide="true">(-1/4)(z+2)(z-4)</function>
  <me>h(z) = $updatingFunc.</me></p>

<p>Now, our first order of business is to solve for the equilibrium points, which occur wherever <m>z_{t+1} = z_t</m>, (or where the graph of the updating function <m>h(z)</m> intersects the diagonal line <m>z_{t+1} = z_t</m>).</p>
<p>To make this statement algebraically, we take the equation <m>z_{t+1}=h(z_t)</m>, set <m>z_{t+1} = z_t = E</m>, and solve the resulting equation for <m>E</m>:</p>

<p>First, replace all instances of <m>z</m> with <m>E</m>:</p>
    <m>E = </m>
    <answer name="ans1a" symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true"><mathInput name="line1" />
    <award>
      <when>$line1 = $$updatingFunc(E)</when>
    </award>
    <award credit="0" name="hOfE">h(E)</award>
  </answer> 
  <feedback condition="$hOfE">You're close.  The equation is <m>E=h(E)</m>.  Now you need to substitute <m>E</m> into the formula for <m>h(z)</m>.</feedback>
  <feedback condition="$ans1a.numSubmissions > 1 and $ans1a.creditAchieved != 1">
    <m>E = $$updatingFunc(E)</m>
  </feedback>
 
  <p>Next, expand the binomial terms on the right hand side into one polynomial:</p>
    <m>E = </m>
    <answer name="ans1b" symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true"><mathInput name="line2" />
    <award>
      <when>$line2 = $$updatingFunc(E)</when>
    </award>
  </answer>
  <feedback condition="$ans1b.numSubmissions > 1 and $ans1b.creditAchieved != 1">
    <m>E = $$updatingFunc(E)</m>
  </feedback>
 
  <p>Subtracting <m>E</m> from both sides gives:</p>
    <m>0 = </m>
    <answer name="ans1c"><mathInput name="line3" />
    <award>
      <when>$line3 = <math simplify="true" name="m3">($$updatingFunc(E)) - E</math></when>
    </award>
  </answer>
  <feedback condition="$ans1c.numSubmissions > 1 and $ans1c.creditAchieved != 1">
    <m>0 = $m3</m>
  </feedback>

  <p>Multiply all terms by <m>-4</m> for clarity:</p>
    <m>0 = </m>
    <answer name="ans1d"><mathInput name="line4" />
    <award>
      <when>$line4 = <math simplify="true" expand="true">-4*($$updatingFunc(E) - E)</math></when>
    </award>
    <award>E^2+2E-8</award>
  </answer>
  <feedback condition="$ans1d.numSubmissions > 1 and $ans1d.creditAchieved != 1">
    <m>0 = <math simplify="true" expand="true">-4*(($$updatingFunc(E)) - E)</math></m>
  </feedback>
  

  <p>Solve for <m>E</m>:</p>
    <m>E = </m> <mathInput name="line5" /> and 
    <m>E = </m> <mathInput name="line6" />
    <answer name="ans1e" matchPartial="true" unorderedCompare="true">
    <award>
      <when>($line5, $line6) = (-4, 2)</when>
    </award>
  </answer>

</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Observe the graph near the equilibria you just found. You can graphically assess the value of the derivative at each equilbrium point by dragging the $tangentLine.styleDescriptionWithNoun. Then, use the <ref to="$stabilityTheorem"><term>Stability theorem</term></ref> to answer the following questions:</p>
  <p>The equilibrium point <m>E_1</m> is 
    <answer inline="true">
    <choice>stable</choice>
    <choice credit="1">unstable</choice>
  </answer>, and the equilibrium point <m>E_2</m> is 
    <answer inline="true">
    <choice credit="1">stable</choice>
    <choice credit="true">unstable</choice>
  </answer>.</p>

  <p>
<booleanInput name="showDiagonal">
  <label>Show Cobwebbing and Diagonal line given by: <m>z_{t+1} = z_t</m></label>
</booleanInput></p>

<p>Updating function: <m>h(z) = </m> $f</p>

  <setup>
    <function name="f" variables="z">$updatingFunc</function>
    <solveEquations variables="z" name="solveForE" minVar="-5" maxVar="3">$f = z</solveEquations>
    <math extend="$A.x" name="px" />
    <derivative name="df">$f</derivative>
    <number name="num">10</number>
    <number name="zeroFixed" fixed="true">0</number>
    <point x="5" y="$initialPoint.x" name="initialPoint" hide="true" labelPosition="left">
      
        <constrainToGraph />
      
      <label>Initial Point</label>
    </point>
    
  </setup>
<!-- <asList>$solveForE.solutions</asList> -->

<!-- 
  <updateValue name="addLine" target="nPoints" newValue="$nPoints+1" type="number">
    <label>Add line</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="deleteLine" target="nPoints" newValue="$nPoints&#x2D;1" type="number" disabled="$nPoints <= 1" >
    <label>Delete line</label>
  </updateValue>
 -->
  <graph identicalAxisScales="true" grid="true" showNavigation="true" name="g1" xmin="-8" xmax="6" ymin="-6" ymax="8">
  <xLabel><m>z_t</m></xLabel>
  <yLabel><m>z_{t+1}</m></yLabel>
  <point name="A" x="0" y="0" styleNumber="2">
    
      <repeat for="$solveForE.solutions" valueName="v"><attractTo><point>($v, $v)</point></attractTo></repeat>
      <constrainTo>$f</constrainTo>
      <constrainToGraph />
    
  </point>
      <line equation="y=$$df($px)(x-$px) + $$f($px)" fixed="true" styleNumber="3" name="tangentLine" applyStyleToLabel="true">
        <label>slope = <m><math displayDigits="2" extend="$tangentLine.slope" /></m></label>
      </line>

    <function extend="$f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" />
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="$num" name="cobwebPolyline" initialPoint="$initialPoint" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" hide="!$showDiagonal" />
    <point extend="$initialPoint" layer="2" hide="!$showDiagonal" />

    <point name="E1" styleNumber="5">($solveForE.solution1, $solveForE.solution1)
      <label><m>E_1</m></label>
    </point>
    <point name="E2" styleNumber="5">($solveForE.solution2, $solveForE.solution2)
      <label><m>E_2</m></label>
    </point>

    
</graph>

</exercise>
</problem>
</section>